Add `Net::Async::MCP::Server::Transport::StreamableHTTP` — the second
transport for the MCP 2026-07-28 architecture. The Stdio transport already
ships; this ticket adds the HTTP/SSE half so web-reachable MCP servers
(load balancers, gateways, browser clients) can talk to the same
`Net::Async::MCP::Server` instance.

# Why

Per the 2026-07-28 spec, Streamable HTTP is the canonical transport for
remote MCP servers. The Stdio transport is fine for local subprocess
clients, but everything else (web UIs, hosted agents, gateway-routed
clients) needs HTTP. The Perl `MCP::Server` ships an HTTP transport; we
should match.

# Spec reference

- [Streamable HTTP, 2026-07-28](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/streamable-http)
- [Custom headers from tool parameters](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/basic/transports/streamable-http#custom-headers-from-tool-parameters)

# Public API

```perl
use Net::Async::MCP::Server;
use Net::Async::MCP::Server::Transport::StreamableHTTP;

my $server = Net::Async::MCP::Server->new(name => 'web-server');
$loop->add($server);

my $http = Net::Async::MCP::Server::Transport::StreamableHTTP->new(
    server        => $server,
    host          => '127.0.0.1',  # default; warns on non-loopback
    port          => 8080,
    path          => '/mcp',       # the single MCP endpoint
    origin_allow  => [],           # empty = allow any; recommend locking down
);
$http->listen;
```

# Wire requirements (from spec)

- Single HTTP **POST** endpoint per spec (`/mcp` by default).
- Honor `Accept: application/json, text/event-stream` from the client.
- Server returns either `application/json` (single object) or
  `text/event-stream` (stream) per request.
- Implementations MUST serve SSE for `subscriptions/listen` (long-lived
  notification stream) and any request that opts into streaming.
- Standard request headers (REQUIRED):
  - `MCP-Protocol-Version` — must match `_meta.io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion`.
  - `Mcp-Method` — mirrors `method`.
  - `Mcp-Name` — mirrors `params.name` (for `tools/call`) or `params.uri`
    (for `resources/read`).
- `x-mcp-header` extension: tool parameters annotated with
  `x-mcp-header: "Foo"` arrive as `Mcp-Param-Foo: <value>` headers.
  Base64-sentinel `=?base64?...?=` for non-ASCII values; server MUST decode
  before comparing to body.
- `Origin` header MUST be checked on every request; invalid → HTTP 403 with
  JSON-RPC error of `id: null`.
- Bind to localhost by default; warn on non-loopback bind.
- Header validation: `MCP-Protocol-Version` != body `_meta.protocolVersion`
  → `-32020 HeaderMismatch` with HTTP 400.
- `Mcp-Name` / `Mcp-Param-*` decoded value must match body param value.
- `Mcp-Method` must match `method`.
- Notifications → HTTP 202 Accepted with no body.

# Implementation choices

- **HTTP server**: PSGI app via `HTTP::Server::PSGI` with `IO::Async::Loop`
  integration. PSGI keeps the test surface small and the impl portable.
  Alternative: `IO::Async::Stream`-based pure-Perl HTTP — port if PSGI
  proves too heavy for the dependency budget.
- **SSE**: implement per spec (`event:`, `data:`, blank-line terminator),
  `X-Accel-Buffering: no`, periodic `:keepalive\r\n` comments.
- **Per-request dispatch**: reuse `Net::Async::MCP::Server::handle()`
  *after* header validation and `_meta` shaping. The handle() method is
  already protocol-agnostic; only the dispatch wrapper changes.
- **Body/header parity check**: in the transport; on mismatch, return
  `-32020 HeaderMismatch` HTTP 400.

# Subtasks

1. **PSGI scaffold.** `Net::Async::MCP::Server::Transport::StreamableHTTP`
   that takes a `server` and returns a PSGI-compatible app. The PSGI body
   is one POST handler that decodes the JSON-RPC envelope, validates
   headers, calls `server->handle()`, and returns the response.
2. **HTTP server bring-up.** Sink to `HTTP::Server::PSGI` on a
   configurable host/port. Reject non-loopback binds with a warning
   unless explicitly enabled.
3. **Standard header validation.** `MCP-Protocol-Version`,
   `Mcp-Method`, `Mcp-Name` (for `tools/call`/`resources/read`/`prompts/get`).
   Body/header parity checks. `-32020 HeaderMismatch` on failure.
4. **Origin validation.** Configurable `origin_allow` list; reject on
   mismatch with HTTP 403 + JSON-RPC error.
5. **`x-mcp-header` parity.** Decode `Mcp-Param-*` headers (Base64
   sentinel handling), compare to body. Reject on mismatch with
   `-32020 HeaderMismatch`.
6. **SSE response mode.** Honor `Accept: text/event-stream`; emit
   `event:`, `data:`, blank-line terminator; `X-Accel-Buffering: no`;
   SSE comment keepalive every 15s on long-lived streams.
7. **`subscriptions/listen` support.** Long-lived SSE stream that
   delivers filter-allowed change notifications. Stub for now; wire
   parity with the spec is the goal.
8. **Notifications.** JSON-RPC notifications (no `id`) → HTTP 202
   Accepted with no body.
9. **Tests.** `t/03-streamable-http.t`:
   - valid POST / `application/json` response
   - valid POST / `text/event-stream` response
   - `MCP-Protocol-Version` mismatch → 400 `-32020`
   - `Mcp-Method` mismatch → 400 `-32020`
   - `Mcp-Name` mismatch → 400 `-32020`
   - `Origin` block → 403
   - `x-mcp-header` parity
   - notification body → 202
   - plain HTTP GET on the endpoint → 405 (per spec)
   - `Accept` enforcement
   - SSE stream lifecycle for `subscriptions/listen`
10. **POD.** Full `[@Author::GETTY]` POD: `=method`s for `listen`, `port`,
    `host`, `path`, `server`; `=attr`s for the same; security section
    pointing at the spec.

# Dependencies

Add to `cpanfile` runtime:
- `HTTP::Server::PSGI`

Add to `cpanfile` on test:
- `Plack` (for `Plack::Test`)
- `HTTP::Request::Common` (for building test requests)
- `LWP::UserAgent` (or `HTTP::Tiny`)

# Out of scope

- TLS — operator terminates via reverse proxy.
- OAuth / Dynamic Client Registration — spec mandates for HTTP; punt to
  a follow-up ticket.
- `HTTP+SSE` (2024-11-05) backwards compatibility — deprecated, skip.

